1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:01,840
We see it as pure poison.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,680
It's the most contradictory of crops.

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:08,520
Now, tobacco on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:25,560
They feed us, clothe us, give us medicines, intoxicate us, stimulate us, provide us with fuel.

5
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:27,360
The list goes on and on.

6
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:32,360
Our relationship to what we grow is usually fairly simple.

7
00:00:32,361 --> 00:00:36,360
If we need a crop, we cultivate it and consume it.

8
00:00:36,361 --> 00:00:38,360
End of story.

9
00:00:38,361 --> 00:00:45,360
But tobacco isn't that simple, because in some ways it's consuming us.

10
00:00:45,361 --> 00:00:54,360
Tobacco's many facets, from the mundane, to the satisfying, to the sinister, make it a very complex crop.

11
00:00:54,361 --> 00:00:56,860
For starters, it's adaptable.

12
00:00:56,861 --> 00:01:02,360
It would be very hard to find a place, short of Antarctica or the Arctic, where it isn't cultivated.

13
00:01:02,361 --> 00:01:04,360
It's profitable.

14
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:11,360
A good average acre of tobacco will bring ballpark figures of $3,500 an acre.

15
00:01:11,361 --> 00:01:16,360
The profitability of tobacco is still the best here of any crop we grow.

16
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:18,360
It's pleasurable.

17
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:20,360
Oh wow. Actually, it smells like food.

18
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:26,200
It's got nuances and I smell cinnamon and a little spice pepper, but it's wonderful.

19
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,500
It's so good, it makes you want to eat it.

20
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,440
It's a tax bonanza.

21
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:36,100
The tobacco industry overall every year pays somewhere in the neighborhood of $100 billion just in taxes.

22
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:42,900
From the seed through the cigarette and into the local municipal state in federal taxes.

23
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:44,320
It's a killer.

24
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:49,720
Smoking is the single largest preventable cause of death in the United States.

25
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:54,300
Tobacco is the only product, if used as directed, will kill you.

26
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:58,280
And it's wildly successful.

27
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:03,180
Whether grown for cigars, pipes, cigarettes, or smokeless tobacco products,

28
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:06,080
six million tons of tobacco are produced each year,

29
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:11,200
with the U.S. ranking fourth in production behind China, India, and Brazil.

30
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:15,500
Tobacco is the largest non-food crop which is grown in the world today.

31
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:19,340
Tobacco is grown in 21 states in the U.S.,

32
00:02:19,341 --> 00:02:22,000
but most production comes from Kentucky and North Carolina.

33
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,540
The U.S. crop is mostly two different types of tobacco,

34
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:29,620
flu-cured and burly,

35
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:32,700
the primary tobaccos found in U.S. blended cigarettes.

36
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:37,760
Burly tobacco is a very high nicotine-type tobacco

37
00:02:37,761 --> 00:02:42,380
that grows in Kentucky and parts of Indiana and Virginia.

38
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:46,080
It's a very scrubby, bushy-type plant.

39
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:53,080
Flu-cured tobacco gets its name from the way in which it's heat dried after harvest.

40
00:02:53,081 --> 00:02:59,080
Unlike Burley, it has low nicotine content, but high sugar levels, which enhance its taste.

41
00:02:59,081 --> 00:03:04,080
Farmers start their crop in mid-February in greenhouses.

42
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:11,080
We come to the field in usually mid-April and we transplant that plant into rows here, like you see in the field.

43
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:14,080
Tobacco requires a lot of nitrogen from the soil.

44
00:03:14,081 --> 00:03:18,080
Farmers will apply 2,000 pounds of fertilizer per acre.

45
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:23,080
Fertilizers will help the plant make sugars, cellulose, and most importantly, nicotine,

46
00:03:23,081 --> 00:03:26,080
the addictive chemical produced by the plant.

47
00:03:26,081 --> 00:03:32,080
Without nicotine in a tobacco plant, it would have virtually little or non-existent commercial use.

48
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:37,080
When a person smokes a cigarette, the tobacco is burned,

49
00:03:37,081 --> 00:03:42,080
and nicotine is distilled off into particles.

50
00:03:42,081 --> 00:03:46,580
These particles contain water, tar, and nicotine.

51
00:03:46,581 --> 00:03:49,580
And these fine particles are inhaled into the lungs,

52
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:54,080
and then they impact or they crash into the very small airways in the lungs.

53
00:03:54,081 --> 00:03:58,080
When these particles impact, nicotine is absorbed pretty quickly

54
00:03:58,081 --> 00:04:01,080
into the bloodstream in the lungs, goes into the heart,

55
00:04:01,081 --> 00:04:03,580
and then goes right out to the body, including to the brain.

56
00:04:03,581 --> 00:04:09,080
It only takes about 15 seconds from taking a puff on a cigarette

57
00:04:09,081 --> 00:04:12,000
to when nicotine has its effects on the brain.

58
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:14,720
There's a concept of rapid reinforcement

59
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,300
that makes a drug more addictive.

60
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:18,300
You know right away what the effect is.

61
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:19,620
You can adjust the dose.

62
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:23,140
And so smoking is actually the most addictive way of taking a drug.

63
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:25,800
But before the tobacco can be smoked,

64
00:04:25,801 --> 00:04:27,120
the plants must mature,

65
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:30,560
growing to be 42 inches high in 60 to 70 days.

66
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:35,520
At this point, they will be harvested gradually.

67
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:38,600
We go out every week or two,

68
00:04:38,601 --> 00:04:41,960
and we'll cut off two or three, four leaves each time we go through.

69
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:46,680
We will end up at the top, which is the best, the highest nicotine,

70
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:50,380
the best plant that the companies want for making cigarettes with.

71
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,800
The leaves must be dried before they can be sold.

72
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,500
We call these bulk barns for drying tobacco.

73
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:03,980
The curing process from start to finish usually takes about seven to eight days.

74
00:05:03,981 --> 00:05:10,160
We'll yellow the leaf up at about 95 to 105 degrees, depending on the outside temperature.

75
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:13,400
Therein lies the term brightly.

76
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:20,880
Once that color is held, then the tobacco is subjected to a pretty intense heat, up to finally 160 degrees.

77
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:26,780
And that's where it stays until all of the moisture has gone out of the leaf, and especially the stems.

78
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:33,440
The tobacco will be stored in 750-pound bales until the tobacco company is ready to receive it.

79
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:39,670
worldwide 5.5 trillion cigarettes are smoked each year.

80
00:05:39,671 --> 00:05:42,980
It's a staggering number, but it

81
00:05:42,981 --> 00:05:48,720
begins mundanely enough with a seed planted, a leaf cut from a stalk, as it has for thousands

82
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:49,712
of years.

83
00:05:49,713 --> 00:05:57,980
Historians believe that tobacco was first cultivated in the Andes, sometime

84
00:05:57,981 --> 00:06:00,380
between 5000 and 3000 BC.

85
00:06:00,381 --> 00:06:07,160
In South America, the tobacco was used in a number of ways which would dazzle us now.

86
00:06:07,161 --> 00:06:10,400
Smoking was only one option of taking it.

87
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:14,540
People sniffed it, they smoked it, they smeared it all over their bodies, they smeared it

88
00:06:14,541 --> 00:06:19,740
on other people's bodies, they drank it in a paste, and they even used it as an enema.

89
00:06:19,741 --> 00:06:25,300
Shamans, spiritual leaders and healers, used it to diagnose illness.

90
00:06:25,301 --> 00:06:28,440
they would consume enough really to take them to the edge of death.

91
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:33,820
And that would take them into the spirit world where they could thereby examine the problems within their patients.

92
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:40,100
But it wasn't until Columbus reached Cuba on October 28, 1492,

93
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:45,100
that Europeans discovered not the gold they sought, but tobacco.

94
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,120
When Columbus sent some of his lieutenants to investigate inland

95
00:06:50,121 --> 00:06:54,120
and try and find a little more about the people who inhabited it and what their habits were,

96
00:06:54,121 --> 00:06:57,520
one of the first matters they reported back was that these people were smoking.

97
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:01,860
And to them it was an absolutely surreal habit.

98
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:04,640
They had no idea, they had no precedent for it in Europe.

99
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:08,900
They came across a tribe who were smoking very large cigars.

100
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:12,780
Tobacco quickly gained new converts.

101
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,860
Its smoke wafted easily across cultural barriers.

102
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:20,780
Spanish and Portuguese sailors picked up the habit and brought tobacco to Europe,

103
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:26,780
where ironically enough, it developed a bogus reputation as a wonder cure for all sorts of ailments.

104
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:33,180
Once the British began visiting North America, they too took up smoking as a recreational habit.

105
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:40,280
John Rolfe is credited as the first English settler to successfully raise tobacco for commercial use

106
00:07:40,281 --> 00:07:42,940
in Jamestown, Virginia in 1612.

107
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:48,900
Since its inception, the colony had been beset by disease and starvation.

108
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:54,380
John Rolfe realized that there could be a potential market for this tobacco

109
00:07:54,381 --> 00:07:56,840
that the natives were growing along the James River.

110
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:00,920
Actually, that is what turned our failure,

111
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:04,280
our fourth failure in settling this continent, into a success.

112
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,740
Tobacco secured the survival of the fledgling colonies.

113
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:09,840
By the end of the 17th century,

114
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:14,280
England was importing more than 20 million pounds of colonial tobacco per year.

115
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,940
We became a tobacco society because tobacco was currency.

116
00:08:17,941 --> 00:08:21,400
It paid for the preachers, it paid taxes, it bought wives.

117
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:27,800
Tobacco was the dollar or the euro of the English settlements in the United States.

118
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:30,340
And tobacco also paid for slaves.

119
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:39,560
The slaves brought into Jamestown in 1619 were brought to help in the cultivation and harvesting and marketing of tobacco.

120
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:45,260
In the 18th century, colonial tobacco farmers, among them George Washington and Thomas Jefferson,

121
00:08:45,261 --> 00:08:47,960
struggled as inequities in the tobacco trade

122
00:08:47,961 --> 00:08:50,020
left them permanently indebted to British merchants.

123
00:08:51,440 --> 00:08:53,240
In tobacco-producing areas,

124
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:56,520
the American Revolution became known as the Tobacco War.

125
00:08:57,640 --> 00:08:59,100
And in 1776,

126
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:02,540
Benjamin Franklin used 5 million pounds of Virginia tobacco

127
00:09:02,541 --> 00:09:04,760
to collateralize a loan from France

128
00:09:04,761 --> 00:09:06,680
to help finance the American Revolution.

129
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:10,580
But whether the world was at war or at peace,

130
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:12,680
as long as there were ships on the sea,

131
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:14,600
tobacco found new customers.

132
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:24,482
Tobacco use spread through Europe and then beyond because it was carried there by the ships of European nations down to Africa, across the Mediterranean, into the

133
00:09:24,483 --> 00:09:25,620
Levant, into Turkey.

134
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:28,044
It spread up the rivers through into Russia.

135
00:09:28,045 --> 00:09:32,020
From Russia it went to China and it was also carried there by the ships of the Portuguese.

136
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:39,920
In the 19th century, tobacco use continued to spread and even crossed battle lines.

137
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:44,200
During the Civil War, the South had tobacco, the North had coffee and food,

138
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:49,380
and many, many times the yank and the rebel would meet in the middle of a path after a battle

139
00:09:49,381 --> 00:09:52,640
and switch tobacco for coffee or tobacco for food.

140
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:57,820
Tobacco in many forms has been exchanged across cultural barriers

141
00:09:57,821 --> 00:10:00,680
between friend and foe and for millennia.

142
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:07,280
But in the 20th century, one form of tobacco, cigarettes,

143
00:10:07,281 --> 00:10:12,260
would become the dominant form of consumption and cause an ongoing health care crisis.

144
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:16,420
But what's actually inside a cigarette?

145
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:19,160
It's a lot more than just tobacco.

146
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:26,500
Tobacco-growing farms in the United States declined from 512,000 in 1954 to 57,000 in 2002.

147
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:34,280
1.3 billion people, one-third of the world's adult population, smoke cigarettes.

148
00:10:34,281 --> 00:10:42,280
tobacco use is rising in the developing world by 3.5 percent per year and falling slightly in the developed world.

149
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:49,280
There are more Chinese smokers than citizens of the United States.

150
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:57,280
In 2005, Philip Morris, the world's largest cigarette company, generated 4.6 billion dollars in profits.

151
00:10:57,281 --> 00:10:57,981
profits.

152
00:10:57,982 --> 00:11:04,640
Cigarettes, by far the most common form of tobacco consumption, are also the most deadly.

153
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:09,880
Unlike most pipe and cigar smokers, cigarette smokers inhale intentionally,

154
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:13,000
allowing toxins to penetrate deeper into the body.

155
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:17,760
There are different risks associated with different forms of smoking.

156
00:11:18,420 --> 00:11:23,140
Cigarette smoking is the one associated with the greatest risk, and that is the one that's

157
00:11:23,141 --> 00:11:28,940
associated mostly with high risks of lung cancer and mouth cancer and chronic lung disease.

158
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:33,520
But if cigarette smoking is so deadly, why is it so popular?

159
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:38,160
A cigarette is an elegant form of a drug delivery device,

160
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:40,500
almost like a hypodermic syringe and needle.

161
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:45,280
And the delivery that one wants out of a cigarette is nicotine.

162
00:11:46,920 --> 00:11:48,920
Nicotine may be the addictive product,

163
00:11:48,921 --> 00:11:52,460
but other cigarette components that accompany it are actually more lethal.

164
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:55,240
So what's inside a U.S. blended cigarette?

165
00:11:56,460 --> 00:12:00,940
Tobaccos, flu-cured, burly, and oriental, only make up about half the cigarette.

166
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:03,080
The rest is known as add-ons.

167
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:08,380
One add-on, reconstituted tobacco, is made from a mixture of stripped stems,

168
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:11,920
ofl, or tobacco dust, swept from the factory floor,

169
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:16,820
and reclaimed, cigarettes that have passed their prime and have been sent back to the factory.

170
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,180
It all goes into a giant vat.

171
00:12:20,181 --> 00:12:26,060
It's beat up. And as it's being beat up, it's constantly extracted like a tea bag.

172
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:33,300
Well, that means I removed all of the chemicals, all the solubles, and then it's pooled into another vat.

173
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:35,840
And this is a chemical reaction vat.

174
00:12:36,860 --> 00:12:39,700
The resulting solution is known as the mother liquor.

175
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:44,380
So you have this mother liquor, which is an aqueous-based system, water-based system,

176
00:12:44,381 --> 00:12:48,220
in which now I can add a host of chemicals.

177
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:52,920
Diamonium phosphate, urea, ammonium hydroxide.

178
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:58,620
And these additives in the recon are used to deal with nicotine manipulation.

179
00:12:59,580 --> 00:13:01,940
The others are thought to use as ameliorance.

180
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:06,380
If you tried to smoke a cigarette without additives, it would be very harsh.

181
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:09,160
How do I mellow that harshness out?

182
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:10,920
How do I smooth it?

183
00:13:10,921 --> 00:13:14,800
Chocolate, butterfat, glycerol.

184
00:13:14,801 --> 00:13:17,920
Sounds a little like things I buy in a grocery store, isn't it?

185
00:13:17,921 --> 00:13:18,920
Or foodstuffs.

186
00:13:18,921 --> 00:13:20,920
Well, they're actually added.

187
00:13:20,921 --> 00:13:25,920
Once chemically reacted, the mother liquor solution is applied to paper made from the

188
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:27,920
ground stems, opal and reclaimed.

189
00:13:27,921 --> 00:13:33,920
This paper is dried and shredded and is known as reconstituted tobacco, or recon.

190
00:13:33,921 --> 00:13:36,920
It will make up 30% of the cigarette's contents.

191
00:13:36,921 --> 00:13:42,920
When it's smoked, the ammonia in the recon will enhance the absorption of nicotine in the smoker's body.

192
00:13:43,920 --> 00:13:50,920
And nicotine as it exists in the plant is sort of like a molecule with a ball and chain on it.

193
00:13:50,921 --> 00:13:55,920
And I want to free this molecule up by taking that ball and chain off.

194
00:13:55,921 --> 00:13:59,920
And now we start changing its shape, we call it freebase nicotine.

195
00:13:59,921 --> 00:14:03,920
Freebasing nicotine is much the same as freebasing cocaine.

196
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:06,640
Cocaine increases potency, increases delivery.

197
00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:13,100
Expanded tobacco, a puff tobacco product that has been expanded with carbon dioxide,

198
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:15,580
is also part of the blend to act as a filler.

199
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:23,440
So in a finished cigarette, 20% is expanded tobacco, stems, and reclaim,

200
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:26,720
tobacco from returned cigarettes, and 30% is recon.

201
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:33,620
The other 50% is tobacco, which is also treated with sugars that mask the bitter taste of nicotine.

202
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:36,060
and enhance its absorption into a smoker's body.

203
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:42,680
Tobacco, stems, reconstituted, reclaimed, and expanded tobacco

204
00:14:42,681 --> 00:14:45,360
are mixed in a bulking bin the size of a bus.

205
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:49,260
It's all cut to 28th of an inch slices, called rag,

206
00:14:49,780 --> 00:14:52,620
and pneumatically moved to the cigarette fabrication side of the plant.

207
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:56,580
There, the rag will be rolled in paper, glued,

208
00:14:57,020 --> 00:14:59,960
joined with filters made from cellulose acetate, and packaged.

209
00:14:59,961 --> 00:15:06,960
This process occurs at a rate of roughly 20,000 cigarettes per minute, sometimes 24 hours a day.

210
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:13,960
Long before offal, casings and recon, cigarettes had humble origins.

211
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:21,960
Their beginnings can be traced to Spain in the early 19th century and the Seville tobacco factory.

212
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:26,960
Cigarettes were rolled from scrap tobacco, often the leftovers from cigars and snuff.

213
00:15:26,961 --> 00:15:33,960
Much of the tobacco produced in Spain in its central monopoly factory was so loose and

214
00:15:33,961 --> 00:15:36,960
that the poorer people would literally roll it up.

215
00:15:36,961 --> 00:15:42,960
In the 19th century, the cigarette's popularity took off with the invention of the first cigarette-making machine.

216
00:15:42,961 --> 00:15:47,960
James Buchanan Duke, known as Buck Duke, saw the future.

217
00:15:47,961 --> 00:15:54,960
In 1884, the Duke Company went into business with James Bonsack, the machine's inventor.

218
00:15:54,961 --> 00:15:59,640
And by the late 1880s, they were manufacturing four million cigarettes daily.

219
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:04,200
Buck Duke was a risk taker and a marketing whiz.

220
00:16:05,720 --> 00:16:10,020
He inserted cigarette cards in the packs with pictures of actresses,

221
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:12,040
which he admitted were just there for prurient interest,

222
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:14,380
all utterly baseball and football stars.

223
00:16:15,020 --> 00:16:18,800
And so he was the one who got cigarettes in front of the nation.

224
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:19,940
And they responded.

225
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:23,140
I mean, smoking rates absolutely shot through the roof.

226
00:16:23,141 --> 00:16:28,240
After mechanization, the movies gave cigarettes their next big boost.

227
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:33,720
One direction cigarette advertising went, of course, was the Hollywood market.

228
00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:39,460
Ronald Reagan, as an example, had his image put on the back of Look and Life.

229
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:46,300
In 1950, around half of the adult population in the industrialized world smoked cigarettes.

230
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:52,440
